WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.910
<v Stephane>So now let's talk about AWS Lambda.</v>

2
00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:04.200
And first its integration.

3
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.480
So there are some main integrations you need to know

4
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:10.560
such as API Gateway to invoke Lambda from Rest API,

5
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.668
Kinesis to read data from a Stream,

6
00:00:12.668 --> 00:00:14.040
DynamoDB to read data

7
00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.629
from a exchange log stream from DynamoDB,

8
00:00:17.629 --> 00:00:19.500
S3 to react to new objects

9
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.810
being created in Amazon S3 for example,

10
00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.000
Internet of Things, EventBridge,

11
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.970
as pretty much a rule output

12
00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:29.850
from any kind of event in EventBridge.

13
00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:33.480
CloudWatch logs, we have SNS to react to notifications,

14
00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.670
Cognito to do some pre

15
00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.490
and post authentication hooks with Lambda

16
00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:41.910
and SQS to read data from a queue.

17
00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:43.590
So here's an example, a very famous one,

18
00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:46.350
which is the Serverless Thumbnail creation.

19
00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.540
So we have a new image in Amazon S3,

20
00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.910
which is going to trigger a Lambda function

21
00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:51.930
to create a thumbnail.

22
00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:55.020
It's going to push the new thumbnail in Amazon S3

23
00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:58.530
and maybe push the metadata of this thumbnail in DynamoDB

24
00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:00.780
that contains the image name, image size,

25
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.520
creation date, and so on.

26
00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.610
Another very common architecture with Lambda

27
00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.940
is to use EventBridge to create a time-based trigger

28
00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.700
to trigger a Lambda function every one hour

29
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.950
to perform a task, effectively giving you a

30
00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:15.633
serverless chron job.

31
00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:18.510
AWS Lambda can run many languages,

32
00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:20.700
such as Node.js for JavaScript,

33
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.320
Python, Java, C#, so either .NET Core or PowerShell,

34
00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.350
Ruby and it supports many other languages

35
00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:29.183
through something called

36
00:01:29.183 --> 00:01:31.200
the custom runtime API, for example.

37
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.620
It supports the Rust or the Golang languages through that.

38
00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.830
You also have the option to use containers on Lambda.

39
00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.810
So this is a container image

40
00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.570
and you must implement what's called the Lambda Runtime API.

41
00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:45.300
Now, that may be too advanced from an exam perspective,

42
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:46.680
but what I need to remember is

43
00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.650
that there is a service named ECS or Fargate.

44
00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:52.830
And so to run container images, especially Docker images,

45
00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:55.710
it is always going to be preferred from an exam perspective

46
00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.530
to run them on ECS or Fargate versus Lambda.

47
00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.060
Even though Lambda supports running

48
00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:03.960
some level of customized Docker images.

49
00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:05.790
So you don't need to remember all the languages

50
00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:06.870
of course from Lambda, but just remember

51
00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.520
that it has some level of support.

52
00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:09.840
The most important ones is going to be

53
00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:13.080
for sure Node.js and Python.

54
00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:15.600
So Lambda will come up in the exam,

55
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.600
especially as a difference, for example,

56
00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:22.200
versus batch versus ECS and other things like this.

57
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.690
And so knowing the limits of Lambda

58
00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:25.890
really helps you understand

59
00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:27.750
whether or not Lambda is a good choice.

60
00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:30.900
So Lambda has up to 10 gigabytes of RAM,

61
00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:32.700
and the CPU is linked to RAM.

62
00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.070
So two vCPUs will be allocated

63
00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:37.620
at about 1800 megabytes of RAM,

64
00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.960
and six vCPUs will be allocated at

65
00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:42.810
around 10 gigabytes of RAM.

66
00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:44.460
Timeout is up to 15 minutes.

67
00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.230
So if you have long executions,

68
00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:47.340
for example one hour,

69
00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.380
Lambda is not a good use case

70
00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:52.410
and the temporary storage is 10 gigabytes.

71
00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:55.860
Here are the deployment packages limits,

72
00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:56.850
50 megabytes zipped,

73
00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:59.670
250 megabytes unzipped, including layers.

74
00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:02.040
You can get up to 1000 concurrent executions,

75
00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.560
although that soft limit can be increased.

76
00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.620
And the max image size for a container is 10 gigabytes

77
00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.720
as well as the invocation payload,

78
00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:10.940
six megabytes when synchronous,

79
00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:15.420
and 256 kilobytes when asynchronous.

80
00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.190
So talking about concurrency,

81
00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:20.970
Lambda has up to 1000 concurrent executions.

82
00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:23.250
So that means that you can go from

83
00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:26.640
having two Lambda executions currently to 1000.

84
00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.590
And you can set a reserved concurrency

85
00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:30.000
at the function level,

86
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.220
which is going to make sure that there is a limit,

87
00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:35.370
but also a reserved capacity for a specific function.

88
00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.828
Now each invocation over the concurrency limit

89
00:03:37.828 --> 00:03:40.020
is going to trigger a throttle.

90
00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:41.160
And so when you're throttling,

91
00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:42.540
you can retry your request.

92
00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.580
And if you get too much throttling,

93
00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.800
then you can request a quota increase

94
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.160
in the AWS service quotas service.

95
00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.800
Now we have a Lambda concurrency issue

96
00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.600
if we don't reserve concurrency.

97
00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.060
So say we have an ALB with Lambda,

98
00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:58.950
we have API gateway with Lambda,

99
00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:02.070
and we have also users using the SDK

100
00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:04.290
or the CLI to invoke Lambda functions.

101
00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:06.030
So this is fine until some day,

102
00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:09.000
your application on the ALB gets very popular

103
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.410
and you have many more users,

104
00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:11.820
and all of a sudden you have

105
00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:15.180
up to 1000 concurrent executions of Lambda,

106
00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.490
and the few users on the API gateway

107
00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:18.840
are going to be throttled

108
00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:21.150
and the users of the SDK or CLI

109
00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:22.740
also will be throttle.

110
00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:25.470
So if you don't reserve concurrency, this can happen.

111
00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:27.159
And so therefore you would reserve concurrency

112
00:04:27.159 --> 00:04:30.600
for the API gateway and reserve concurrency for the SDK

113
00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.248
and that you would not run into this issue

114
00:04:32.248 --> 00:04:34.350
because there will be a minimum

115
00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:35.790
number of Lambda functions

116
00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:38.733
that can run for your API gateway and so on.

117
00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:42.410
You can also deploy Lambda functions with CodeDeploy.

118
00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:44.970
So CodeDeploy will help you do traffic shift

119
00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:47.430
between two Lambda aliases or versions.

120
00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:49.050
And so this feature is integrated directly

121
00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:50.610
within the SAM framework.

122
00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:51.990
So say you have a prod alias

123
00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:54.420
pointing to the V1 and the V2 function.

124
00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.970
Using code deploy, you can shift, gradually,

125
00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:59.100
the traffic from V1 to V2

126
00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:02.250
by having first 100% on V1 and 0% on V2,

127
00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:05.760
and then slowly going 80/20 and so on.

128
00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:06.593
So for this is CodeDeploy.

129
00:05:06.593 --> 00:05:10.110
CodeDeploy will make that proportion vary over time.

130
00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:11.070
So you have different strategies.

131
00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:13.170
You have the linear to grow traffic

132
00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:15.450
every N number of minutes until 100%.

133
00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:16.800
So it's linear growth.

134
00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:19.560
So you have, for example, 10% every three minutes

135
00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.420
or 10% every 10 minutes.

136
00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:23.040
And you can try Canary as well

137
00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:27.300
to try a small amount of traffic for say five minutes.

138
00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:28.530
So 10% for five minutes,

139
00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:30.870
and then if everything is stable

140
00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:32.730
and no rollback happens,

141
00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:34.920
then switch all the traffic over.

142
00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:37.948
So it's a bit quicker and you just test a small subset,

143
00:05:37.948 --> 00:05:39.390
that's what it's called Canary.

144
00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:41.280
So you get Canary, 10%, five minutes,

145
00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:44.040
or Canary, 10%, 30 minutes, and so on.

146
00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:45.510
And if you don't want to transition

147
00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:46.620
from V1 to V2,

148
00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:47.610
you can do all at once

149
00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:49.560
to get immediate traffic shift

150
00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:51.450
between your two Lambda versions.

151
00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:52.920
You can also run pre

152
00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.837
and post traffic hooks to check the health

153
00:05:54.837 --> 00:05:56.400
of your Lambda function

154
00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.230
so that you don't move over

155
00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.570
all the traffic to the new one after a Canary

156
00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:03.120
if you don't validate some kind of health checks

157
00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:05.043
and posts traffic hooks.

158
00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:07.290
Now regarding monitoring,

159
00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:08.760
so CloudWatch is going to be good

160
00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:11.010
because you're going to get all your execution logs

161
00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:12.450
in CloudWatch logs.

162
00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:13.750
And also CloudWatch metrics,

163
00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:15.360
you're going to get all information

164
00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:18.390
around successful invocations, error rates,

165
00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:20.370
latency, timeouts, and so on.

166
00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:21.480
And so for this, you need to make sure

167
00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:23.275
that Lambda has an execution role

168
00:06:23.275 --> 00:06:27.300
that allows access to CloudWatch logs, for example.

169
00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:29.070
Now for X-ray, it's possible

170
00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:32.280
to trace Lambda functions invocations with X-ray.

171
00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:33.840
And so for this, you just enable it

172
00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:35.070
in the Lambda configuration

173
00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:36.990
to run the X-ray daemon for you.

174
00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:38.550
And then in your code you would add

175
00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:42.150
the tracing mechanism directly using the AWS SDK.

176
00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:43.410
For this, again, you need to make sure

177
00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:47.520
that your Lambda function has correct access to X-ray.

178
00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:49.320
So that's it for the first part of Lambda.

179
00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:52.320
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

